  "detail": null,
  "summary": "Daniel Ferreira (pt-0015): placed the contrast CT and handled the RBC-RAD-07 prep. One thing for you to look at: his iohexol allergy entry lists \"anaphylaxis\" from his own account in 2021, but the 2019 radiology report doesn't support it. I left the entry unchanged.\n\n- **CT order:** CT abdomen and pelvis with IV contrast (sr-t0001) for Mon 10/26 at 9:00 at St. Brigid. It's linked to the booked appointment (appt-t5-1026) per §5.\n- **Allergy grading (§1):** The radiologist's report from the day of the 2019 iohexol reaction calls it moderate: hives and eyelid swelling, no airway or breathing symptoms, stable vital signs. Policy says to grade by that day's record, so the later \"anaphylaxis\" label doesn't change it. That means premedication applies (§2), not the no-contrast rule for severe reactions (§3). His shellfish, povidone-iodine, aspirin and mold allergies don't count as contrast risks.\n- **Premedication (§2):**\n  - Prednisone 50 mg by mouth at 8 PM Sunday 10/25, 2 AM Monday and 8 AM Monday (mr-t0003).\n  - Diphenhydramine 50 mg by mouth at 8 AM Monday (mr-t0004).\n- **Kidney check (§4):** He needs a creatinine within 30 days before the scan because he's 64 and has diabetes. His last one (1.02 mg/dL on 9/24) is 32 days before the scan, so it doesn't count. I ordered a new one (sr-t0002), to be drawn between today and Monday 8:30 AM.\n- **Patient message (comm-t0005):** Tells him to:\n  - get the blood test before the scan\n  - follow the medication times above\n  - have someone drive him, since he can't drive after the diphenhydramine\n  - arrive by 8:45\n  - expect possibly higher blood sugar on prednisone\n  - tell the CT staff about his 2019 reaction\n  - call 911 for any reaction symptoms\n- **Follow-up task (task-t0006):** Assigned to you, due Monday 8:30 AM: check the creatinine was drawn and reviewed before the scan, and that he has his medicines and a driver.\n- **Chart note (doc-t0007):** Documents all of the above and cites RBC-RAD-07.",
